|~ The verbs of strings [D62]. Each resides on `::qlang/string`, the
   kind whose module declares it [D72]: the comparisons, which compare
   two strings by their code units in the one order [D65], the verbs
   that read a string, and `prepend` and `append`, under the contracts
   of their names on `::qlang/any`. ~|

:gt
  |~~ Whether the subject follows the string the call gives.

      ~("b" | gt "a" | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::string | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/gt})

:gte
  |~~ Whether the subject follows or equals the string the call gives.

      ~("b" | gte "b" | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::string | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/gte})

:lt
  |~~ Whether the subject precedes the string the call gives.

      ~("a" | lt "b" | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::string | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/lt})

:lte
  |~~ Whether the subject precedes or equals the string the call gives.

      ~("a" | lte "a" | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::string | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/lte})

:split
  |~~ The substrings between the occurrences of the separator, as a
      vector. Inverse of `join`.

      ~("a,b,c" | split "," | eq ["a" "b" "c"])
      ~("line1\nline2" | split "\n" | eq ["line1" "line2"])
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:separator ::string | :returns ::vec | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/split})

::SplitSubjectNotStringError
  |~~ Captured argument at position 1 of `split` must be String.
      ~(
        42 | split ","
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::SplitSubjectNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::SplitSeparatorNotStringError
  |~~ Captured argument at position 2 of `split` must be String.
      ~(
        "abc" | split 42
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::SplitSeparatorNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:lines
  |~~ The lines of a text, as a vector of strings. A `\n` ends a line
      and a `\r` before it belongs to the ending; a newline at the end
      closes the last line rather than opening an empty one, so a text
      reads alike with and without it, and the empty text has no lines.
      The text is then worked on as the vector it has become, and
      `join "\n"` writes it back.

      ~("north\nsouth" | lines | eq ["north" "south"])
      ~("one\r\ntwo\n" | lines | eq ["one" "two"])
      ~("" | lines | eq [])
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::vec | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/lines})

::LinesSubjectNotStringError
  |~~ Subject of `lines` must be String.
      ~(
        42 | lines
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::LinesSubjectNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:contains
  |~~ Whether the string holds the needle as a substring; the empty
      needle is always held. Case-sensitive.

      ~("hello world" | contains "world" | eq true)
      ~("hello" | contains "xyz" | eq false)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:needle ::string | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/contains})

::ContainsSubjectNotStringError
  |~~ Captured argument at position 1 of `contains` must be String.
      ~(
        42 | contains "x"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::ContainsSubjectNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::ContainsNeedleNotStringError
  |~~ Captured argument at position 2 of `contains` must be String.
      ~(
        "hi" | contains 42
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::ContainsNeedleNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:startsWith
  |~~ Whether the string begins with the prefix; the empty prefix
      always begins it. Case-sensitive.

      ~("hello world" | startsWith "hello" | eq true)
      ~("hello" | startsWith "world" | eq false)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:prefix ::string | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/startsWith})

::StartsWithSubjectNotStringError
  |~~ Captured argument at position 1 of `startsWith` must be String.
      ~(
        42 | startsWith "x"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::StartsWithSubjectNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::StartsWithPrefixNotStringError
  |~~ Captured argument at position 2 of `startsWith` must be String.
      ~(
        "hi" | startsWith 42
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::StartsWithPrefixNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:endsWith
  |~~ Whether the string ends with the suffix; the empty suffix always
      ends it. Case-sensitive.

      ~("hello world" | endsWith "world" | eq true)
      ~("hello" | endsWith "xyz" | eq false)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:suffix ::string | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/endsWith})

::EndsWithSubjectNotStringError
  |~~ Captured argument at position 1 of `endsWith` must be String.
      ~(
        42 | endsWith "x"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::EndsWithSubjectNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::EndsWithSuffixNotStringError
  |~~ Captured argument at position 2 of `endsWith` must be String.
      ~(
        "hi" | endsWith 42
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::EndsWithSuffixNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:prepend
  |~~ The string with the prefix in front; a tag over the string comes
      back on the answer.

      ~("world" | prepend "hello " | eq "hello world")
      ~(::Box("b") | prepend "a" | eq ::Box("ab"))
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:prefix ::string | :returns / | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/prepend})

::PrependPrefixNotStringError
  |~~ Captured argument at position 2 of `prepend` must be String.
      ~(
        "x" | prepend 42
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::PrependPrefixNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:append
  |~~ The string with the suffix after it; a tag over the string comes
      back on the answer.

      ~("hello" | append " world" | eq "hello world")
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:suffix ::string | :returns / | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/append})

::AppendSuffixNotStringError
  |~~ Captured argument at position 2 of `append` must be String.
      ~(
        "x" | append 42
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::AppendSuffixNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:parseJson
  |~~ The qlang value a string of plain JSON spells, the reverse of
      `json`: the keys of an object become keywords, an array a vector,
      and scalars pass through. Lossy for the shapes of qlang alone: a
      set arriving from JSON reads as a vector, a keyword as a value as
      a string, and errors have no plain form. It reads input from any
      producer that speaks JSON.

      ~("42" | parseJson | eq 42)
      ~("\"hello\"" | parseJson | eq "hello")
      ~("[1,2,3]" | parseJson | eq [1 2 3])
      ~("{\"name\":\"alice\"}" | parseJson | eq {:name "alice"})
  ~~|
  ::verb~(::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/parseJson})

::ParseJsonSubjectNotStringError
  |~~ Subject of `parseJson` must be a String — the raw JSON text;
      a value renders to one through `json` first.

      ~(
        42 | parseJson
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::ParseJsonSubjectNotStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::ParseJsonInvalidJsonError
  |~~ `parseJson` could not parse the subject as JSON. The message
      of the host's parser rides on `:message`.

      ~("{not json" | parseJson !| type | eq ::ParseJsonInvalidJsonError)
  ~~|

:keyword
  |~~ The keyword the string names, bridging the world of strings, JSON
      data and text built from parts, with the world of keywords, the
      keys of maps and the discriminators of enums.

      ~("foo" | keyword | eq :foo)
      ~("qlang/error" | keyword | eq :qlang/error)
      ~("bar" | keyword | keyword | eq "bar")
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::keyword | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/keyword})

:parse
  |~~ The quote of the steps the source text spells, the reverse of
      `parse` on a quote. Pair it with `apply /` to evaluate source
      text without leaving qlang. A source the parser refuses answers a
      `::ParseError` on the fail-track, which `!|` reads like every
      other error.

      ~("1 | add 1" | parse | count | eq 2)
      ~("add 1 2" | parse | eq ~(add 1 2))
  ~~|
  ::verb~(::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/parse})
